          <article id="exercise-01" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">
              01
            </div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div>
                  <span class="concept-tag">
                    Controller orientation · code trace
                  </span>
                  <h2>
                    Command the motors from the StampC3
                  </h2>
                </div>
                <span class="time-badge">
                  20–30 min
                </span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                  <strong>Goal:</strong> establish that you can use code to deliberately start and stop simple motor movements.  The main outcome is to become familiar with the general layout of the code base.<br><br> 
                  
                  After following the <a href="getting-started.html">Getting Started</a> exercises, you should have a renamed copy of the <strong>StampC3_Template</strong> on your computer - this is a good starting point.  Reviewing the files, the <code>.ino</code> file is the <strong>top-level</strong> file - this means it essentially contains and structures all other code used.  Therefore, the .ino file is the correct starting point to review and understand the code.</p>
                  
                  <p>The original StampC3_Template has an example line-following controller that uses surface-sensor information to make decisions to one command each motor.  This is more complicated than we currently need, but it serves as a good example to recognise how to use functions, and the patterns of code that produce robot control.</p>
                  <p>When we develop our later test routines we will also need to decide when to start and stop the motors.  As a first exercise, the easiest way to start is to edit existing code.  We want to build some familiarity with the code used to operate the robot. </p>
                  
                <p>First, observe in StampC3_Template.ino (the top level file) that the function <strong>loop()</strong> contains the line of code:
                <blockquote>controller.update(robot, server);</blockquote>
                This is the first clue about how the code controls the robot.  Reading this line of code, controller is being used to update the robot.  At the top of the same file, you will find the line:
                <blockquote>Controller_c controller;</blockquote>
                This line creates a <strong>global variable</strong> named controller, which has the datatype Controller_c.  Because controller is defined with global scope (outside of all functions), this means that controller will exist in the computer memory for a long as the robot is powered on.  Controller_c is a <strong>class</strong>, which can be understood to mean that it collects together a set of variables and functions.  To access the variables and functions within a class, the dot operator (.) is used.  Hence, controller.update(...) is calling a function defined within Controller_c.  
                </p>
                <p>To learn more about what functionality Controller_c currently provides, review the file <strong>Controller.h</strong>.  Our current focus is to operate the motors. In Controller.h, find every call to:<blockquote>robot.setMotorPWM(...)</blockquote> 
                  The above line of code can be read from right-to-left to help us understand what it does and where it is defined in the code.  When you are presented with a lot of code to work with, you will have to behave like a detective to develop your familiarity and undersatnding.</p>
                  
                  <p>Reading the line of code: first, <strong>setMotorPWM(...)</strong> is a function, which we can identify by the parentheses.   The name of the function describes setting the <strong>pulse width modulation</strong> (PWM) for the motors.  PWM is the name given to a technique to approximate an analogue output on a digital output pin (i.e., graduated values between on and off).  A digital output pin can only output a high (typically 5v or 3.3v, depending on the microcontroller) or low value (0v).  If we could only use a digital output, the motors would be powered either fully-on, or off.</p>
                  <p>  To provide continuous command of a motor (varied power levels), PWM very quickly alternates between high and low values in repetition, generating a square waveform on the output pin.  To change the approximate voltage on the digital pin, the pulse (high period) is varied with respect to the low period.   A long high period generates a higher voltage, whereas a longer low period generates a lower voltage.</p> 
                  
                  <p>Next, the dot (.) can be read as "contained within" the preceding object, <strong>robot</strong>.  The dot operator is used to access functions and variables within an object.  To learn more about <strong>robot</strong>, the next step of investigation is to find where robot is declared. You will find the following line of code at the top of the StampC3_Template.ino file:
                  <blockquote>Robot_c robot;</blockquote>
                  This line tells us that robot has the datatype <strong>Robot_c</strong> - robot is the name to access this memory, and Robot_c is the definition of how the memory is structured for the datatype.  The _c suffix is a convention adopted throughout the StampC3_Template code, and it can be read to mean that this datatype is a <strong>class</strong>.  In other areas, _t will be used to mean a unique type, and _s to mean a struct datatype.</p>  
                  
                      <aside class="teaching-callout">
                      <h3>Discuss and Agree Subjective Choices in Code</h3>
                  
                  <p>These conventions have been adopted to help with the readablity of the code.  Modern code editors will highlight common datatypes such as float, int, char, to help you recognise a datatype.  However, datatypes newly created are unlikely to be highlighted and can quickly become a source of confusion or bugs.</p>
                  <p>For example, if we simply used <strong>r</strong> as the name of the class (<code>r robot;</code>), it is not immediately clear that this is a datatype, nor whether it is a class, structure, or something else - and it doesn't provide any clues about where to find out more information.  Using r to <i>define</i> a datatype could easily become confused with a variable <i>named</i> r elsewhere.</p>
                  <p>You can create your own conventions.  In the future, you may be required by your employer to follow a set of conventions.  Some conventions prefix whilst other suffix.  If you use Generative AI, it is likely to adopt conventions, and these may vary depending on the model, session or context.  In any case, it is important to discuss and agree any conventions with your team. If you don't do this, the ability of your team to cooperate and support each other will rapidly degrade.</p>
                  <p>If you want to see how extremely unreadable code can become, you could review the submissions to the <a href="https://www.ioccc.org/index.html">The International Obfuscated C Code Contest</a> (<a href="https://github.com/ioccc-src/winner/blob/master/2025/ayu/prog.c">example</a>).  You can read more about <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coding_conventions">code conventions</a> and <a href="https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Computer_Programming/Coding_Style">code styles</a>.</p> 
</aside>                  
                     
                  
                  <p>You can review <strong>Robot.h</strong> to find the definition of the Robot_c class.  Spend some time to explore how Robot_c is defined, and what other functionalities it offers.  You should review each of the functions - the functions within Robot.h all concern the fundamental operation of the Pololu 3Pi+ robot.  You may feel this is overwhelming at first - to make this easier, you can consider it a process of exploring and mapping, getting familiar.</p> 
                  
                  <p>Returning to Controller.h find the controller <strong>update(...)</strong>  function,  and then follow the code execution path into <strong>runLineFollower(...)</strong>. Compare the commands used while the robot is waiting, following the line, searching for the line, and stopped. Notice that the <strong>arguments</strong> to the setMotorPWM(...) function change depending on when and how it is being used.</p>
                  
				
                  <p>In these exercises, it is <strong>strongly recommended that you keep your robot upside-down on the table</strong> so that the robot does not run away at high speed and <strong>cause damage</strong>.
                </p>
              </div>
