          <article id="exercise-02" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">
              02
            </div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div>
                  <span class="concept-tag">
                    Encoder data · speed measurement
                  </span>
                  <h2>
                    Measuring Wheel Speed: Encoders
                  </h2>
                </div>
                <span class="time-badge">
                  25–35 min
                </span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                <aside class="teaching-callout">
                <h3>Stripped Gears</h3>
                <p>Please do not stop the wheel rotation with your finger or by holding the robot still.</p>
                <p>The motors of the robot contain a small gear train that is quite fragile.  If you stop the rotation of the wheel whilst the motor is powered, the motor will strip the teeth from the gears.  This will cause permanent damage to the robot.</p>
                <p>  It is also possible to cause similar damage by pushing the robot along a surface at high speed.</p> 
                </aside>
		             Using <strong>robot.setMotorPWM(...)</strong> sets the power to the motors, it does not control the <strong>speed</strong>. This is immediately clear if you consider that you can offer some light resistance to the wheel rotation with your finger and the wheel slows down.  In later exercises, we will characterise the motor by measuring it's speed.  Therefore, we need to learn how to detect and measure the wheel speed.</p>  
                  
                  <p>Each motor on the robot is fitted with a quadrature encoder.  The encoder has a disc magnet which rotates above a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hall_effect_sensor">Hall Effect sensor</a>.  This means that when the motor shaft rotates an electronic pulse is created that can be counted.  More information on how the encoders work can be found in the <a href="https://www.pololu.com/docs/0J83/5.4">official documentation</a>.  In this course, counting the pulses and determining whether the wheel is rotating forwards or backwards has been implemented for you.  The robot will register a change in wheel rotation and either increment or decrement the rotation count.
                </p>
                <p>To read the current encoder count for the motors, we will utilise a helper function within the <strong>Robot_c</strong> class (defined in Robot.h). In Robot_c, find 
                <blockquote>getEncoders()</blockquote>  Reviewing the code in this function, we can observe that the function requests a "snapshot".  You can find this snapshot (encoders) and other snapshot variables declared at the top of the Robot_c class.  This snapshot terminology reflects the fact that the encoder values retrieved were correct at the time they were requested, but that they may have since changed.  In real terms, if a snapshot of the encoder counts is retrieved, but the robot continues to move, then we expect that the count stored on the robot itself will have continued updating.  We will actually use this property to allow the robot to estimate wheel speed.</p>
                
                <p>Two more helper functions, <strong>getLeftEncoderCount()</strong>, and <strong>getRightEncoderCount()</strong>, are provided to attempt to reduce potential confusion about which count represents which wheel.  Reviewing the function getLeftEncoderCount(), we can observe that it has the <strong>return type</strong> of <strong>int32_t</strong>.  This means that the function will return a <strong>signed integer</strong> number value, whose range is represented across <strong>32 bits</strong>.  In actuality, 1 bit is reserved to represent the sign of the number (-/+) whilst the remaining 31 bits represent the numeric value.  Suffice to say, an int32_t can store very large number values.</p>
                
                <p>Counts of rotation produced by the encoder may seem confusing - how does this map to a rotation or distance travelled?  The official documentation for the <a href="https://www.pololu.com/docs/0J83/5.4">encoders</a> tells us that one whole rotation of the wheel produces 358.3 encoder counts.  We can therefore work out either how much rotation occurs for 1 encoder count ( degrees: \(\frac{360}{358.3}\), or radians: \(\frac{2\pi}{358.3}\)), or how much distance would be travelled by the wheel for one encoder count (wheel circumference divided by 358.3).  </p> 
                <p>To estimate a wheel speed, we will apply the simple speed formula:
                 <div class="equation">
                \(v = d / t\)
                </div> 
                where \(v\) is velocity, \(d\) is distance, and \(t\) is time.  To use this equation, where therefore need our robot to identify values of \(d\) and \(t\).</p>
                <p>For time, we can refer back to exercise 1.  In exercise 1 you used <strong>TaskTimer_t</strong> to schedule a task to operate at a time interval.  By using the same mechanism, we can request the encoder counts at a specific time interval, \(t\).  Next, we want to know how far the wheel rotated (or travelled along a surface), \(d\), within that same time interval.  To do this, we will need to capture the encoder counts before and after the time interval.  </p>
                
                <p>We know that the controller code is iterative - meaning, it keeps repeating or looping.   Therefore, we can actually use the encoder count at time \(t-1\) (last time step) as the reference for the next encoder count at time \(t\) (current time step).  Our distance measurement is fundamentally the difference between two encoder counts.  The delta symbol, \(\Delta\), is commonly used to mean the difference: </p>
                <div class="equation">
                \(\Delta e^{left} = e^{left}_{t-1} - e^{left}_{t} \\ 
                \Delta e^{right} = e^{right}_{t-1} - e^{right}_{t}\)
                </div>
                <p>Once you have \(\Delta e^{left}\) and \(\Delta e^{right}\), you can choose whether to transform these into units of rotation (e.g., radians per second) or distance (e.g., mm per second).  You can also choose the resolution of units based on what feels appropriate for your system - for example, mm/s, cm/s, m/s, and so on.</p>
                <p>In the following steps, it is best to start by working on one motor only.  Once you have a speed estimation working reliably for one motor, it should be relatively simple to extend this for both motors.</p> 
                
              </div>
              <div class="exercise-grid">

                <div class="context-box" data-context-box>
                  <div class="context-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Encoder speed measurement guidance">
                    <button class="context-tab active" data-context="nonai" role="tab" aria-selected="true">
