          <article id="exercise-03" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">
              03
            </div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div>
                  <span class="concept-tag">
                    Threshold discovery · static test
                  </span>
                  <h2>
                    Deadband: find where motion begins
                  </h2>
                </div>
                <span class="time-badge">
                  25–35 min
                </span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                  <strong>Goal:</strong> use the encoder-speed measurement developed in Exercise 2 to determine whether each motor has a low-PWM command region in which no reliable wheel motion can be detected.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Using robot.setMotorPWM(...) will generate a PWM signal for the left and right motors. At small command magnitudes, the available motor torque may be insufficient to overcome static friction in the motor, gearbox, bearings, tyre contact, or other properties of the task environment. Driver electronics, command handling, and the effective PWM duty cycle can also contribute. The resulting low-PWM command region, where changing the value produces no detectable sustained motion, is commonly described as a <strong>deadband</strong>.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Deciding that motion has begun is not as simple as observing a change of one encoder count. A wheel may twitch and stop, begin moving only after a delay, or rotate so slowly that a short measurement time interval records no count change. Encoder resolution and timing therefore affect what you can detect.  Establishing an exact deadband threshold is therefore very difficult.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Deadband is associated with starting from rest. Once the motor and wheel has started moving consistently, it will have overcome static friction and require less power to operate.  Therefore, the command that first causes a stationary wheel to turn may be larger than the command required to keep that wheel turning once it is already moving. These are breakaway and continued-motion behaviours.  It is for this reason that measuring deadband is not as simple as sweeping a PWM value from 0 to maximum - each PWM value needs testing from a stopped initial state.</p>
                  
              <figure class="motor-diagram">
                <figcaption>
                  Figure 1. An idealised centred deadband across the supported −400 to +400 PWM command range; the threshold positions are illustrative
                </figcaption>
                <img class="deadband-illustration" src="assets/images/motors/motor_deadband.png" width="100%" alt="Plot of normalised wheel response against requested motor PWM from minus 400 to plus 400. The response remains zero in a shaded deadband centred on zero between separate negative and positive thresholds, then increases outside those thresholds." />
              </figure>
                  <p>It is also important to note that a deadband region may also differ between the left and right motors, and between forward and reverse rotation. Robot support, weight distribution, surface contact, mechanical load, and the time allowed at rest can change the result.  In this exercise, you should evaluate both motors independently.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Deadband matters in robot control, and particularly in Digital Twin systems. When deadband exists, a controller that assumes a small non-zero command creates a proportional movement will be wrong. On a real robot, this discontinuity in the relationship between power and motion can create stuttering or the inability to move at low velocity.  In simulation, assuming that a deadband does not exist would mean that any controllers developed in simulation are unlikely to perform as expected when transferred to a real robot.</p>
                <p>
                  In this exercise your objective is to determine the lowest PWM value that can generate consistent wheel rotation.  It is important to conduct your tests with the robot starting at rest (no motor activation).  This is to ensure that the motor activation must overcome static friction.  These tests can be conducted manually - meaning, simple setting a PWM value, observing the result, and then reprogramming the robot with the next PWM value.  However, it is recommended that you instead pursue an automated method, where your robot will test a set of PWM values for itself, and store the corresponding wheel speed registered.
                </p>
                <figure class="motor-diagram">
                <figcaption>
                  Figure X
                </figcaption>
                <p align="center">
                <img src="assets/images/motors/deadband_pseudocode.png" width="75%" alt="" />
                </p>
              </figure>
                <p>
                The above pseudocode describes a general test process.  At the top of the pseudocode, some variables are identified and you will need decide their values. These are the starting PWM value  to test (\(PWM_{min}\)), the last PWM value to test (\(PWM_{max}\)) and the increment value (\(\Delta PWM\)) to test PWM values between start and last values.  You should also decide how much time is required to allow the robot to come to a full stop (resting, \(T_{rest}\)), and how much time the robot should be given with a motor activated (\(T_{run}\)).</p> 
         
         <p>Within the the time period \(T_{run}\), we want the robot to collect a quantity of  measurements (\(N_{s}\)).  Line 9 indicates a relationship between \(T_{run}\) and \(N_{s}\): the larger \(N_{s}\), the smaller the time between samples (\(\Delta t\)).  The update(...) function of the Controller_c class is configure to operate every 10ms.  Keeping within the framework presented by the Controller_c class, you should therefore assume that the smallest sample interval is 10ms.</p>
         
         <p>Lines 8 to 12 of the pseudocode are presented as <strong>blocking</strong> code operation.  We can identify this by the <code>wait</code> instruction on line 9.  Waiting for some time to elapse can be achieved with the <code>delay()</code> function in Arduino (<a href="https://docs.arduino.cc/language-reference/en/functions/time/delay/">reference page</a>).  However, using delay will mean that your StampC3 cannot complete any other work.  In the future, this will present a problem when the robot is required to complete many activities quickly, such as reading sensors and controlling motors.</p>    
         <p>To start with, your test function can adopt the simpler blocking approach specified in the above pseudocode. You are encouraged to then refactor this routine into a <strong>non-blocking</strong> version to become comfortable and familiar with the approach.</p>   
         <p>Line 11 has the operation <code>Report (<i>pwm, trial, sample, speed</i>)</code>.  Therefore, at each iteration of your code, we would expect to view on the Serial Monitor an output like:
         <blockquote>25, 1, 1, 0.16<br>25, 1, 2, 0.13<br>25, 1, 3, 0.15<br>25, 1, 4, 0.14<br>25, 1, 5, 0.11<br>25, 2, 1, 0.15<br>...</blockquote>
					In the above example output, each row is a reported result from an iteration of the code.  We can see that the final value (speed) is fluctuating. The first value is constant - that is because it is PWM 25 that is currently being tested.  The second value remains at 1 (first trial), until the sample reaches 5, and then trial increments to 2 and sample resets to 1.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="exercise-grid">

                <div class="context-box" data-context-box>
