                    <li>
                      <strong>Select the new test.</strong> Change the single test-function call in <code>update(...)</code> to <code>testDeadband(robot, now)</code>.  
                      <ol>
                      <li>You are strongly encouraged to keep using the Controller_c class framework.  This is because the Controller_c class is intended to later allow you to transfer code between the real robot and the Digital Twin simulation.  Therefore, you should allow loop() to call controller.update(), and then allow update() to call your new function.</li>
                      <li>By adopting the Controller_c framework, you code will also wait for a button press before it begins.  This will allow you to start your tests when you are ready, rather than your robot surprising you.</li>
	                   <li>In your first attempt at this exercise, you are guided to implement the blocking approach specified in the pseudocode above.  Importantly, this means that whilst the controller.update() function is configured to run every 10ms, your function will take considerably longer to complete - it will block.  Once you have the blocking version working, you should refactor your code to achieve the same test functionality, but as non-blocking code.  This means your function should complete just one iteration, and return to update() to be called again.</li>
                      
                      </ol>
                    </li>
                      
                      
                      <li><strong>Implement the pseudocode</strong> Using the pseudocode as a schematic, implement the while and for loops specified.  You will need to add variable declarations to your function for \(PWM_{min}, PWM_{max}\) and so on.  
                      <ol>
                      <li>Because we are starting with a blocking approach, line 9 can be achieved with the delay() function.</li> 
                      </ol>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Run the physical trials.</strong> Begin with one wheel and one direction.  By activating just one motor, your robot should move in a small circle.  Whilst solving problems with your code, it will probably be better to flip your robot upside down so that it doesn't move.   
                      <ol>
                      <li>In the exercises concerning the motors, the USB cable is likely to become inconvenient, or even interfere with your results.  You can switch from Serial.print() to server.printf() to report results over a Wi-Fi connection, and so remove the USB cable. You will need to follow the steps to connect your computer to your robot, which hosts the Wi-Fi network.</li>
                      <li><strong>Verify:</strong> check that the value you have given to \(T_{rest}\) is long enough for the robot to come to a complete stop.  Check that \(T_{run}\) and \(N_{s}\) are interacting in the way you intended - that is, the correct number of samples within the period, and the correct sample time interval.</li>
                      </ol>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Calculate and plot.</strong> Plot the estimated wheel speed against requested PWM.  Annotate the plot at the point of the PWM command with no sustained motion and the first command with sustained motion. 
                      <ol>
                      <li>It is expected that on the first attempt, your dataset will give a rough indication of the deadzone, but more data collection will be necessary.  This is a normal experience.  First tests are initially coarse in their resolution to first identify the region of interest.  Once this has been established, the tests can be repeated with a smaller range focused on the region of interest, and with a finer resolution of increments.  You should repeat your tests to improve your estimates of the deadband zone and threshold values.</li>
                      </ol>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Test assumptions: </strong> If you have tested only the positive PWM values, you should go back to test the negative PWM values.  Similarly, if you have only captured tests for one motor, you should now conduct tests for the other motor.  Within your team, you will have 3 robots.  It would also be good to test how deadband varies between the robots.
                    </li>
                    <li><strong>Refactor for Non-Blocking:</strong> As mentioned in the introduction to this exercise, the pseudocode presents a simpler blocking approach to this test process.  To refactor your code to a non-blocking approach, the following steps must be undertaken:
                    <ol>
                    <li>A non-blocking approach requires your test function to return to controller.update() after 1 iteration.  Therefore, any variables declared within your test function (local variables) will be deleted and lose their value.</li>
                    <li>To maintain a count of <i>trial</i> and <i>sample</i>, these variables will need to be declared outside the function, but within the Controller_c class.  This will make them "global" to the class, and so persistent (keep their values).  </li> 
                    <li>Similarly, to track which PWM value is being tested, this variable will also need to be declared at the Controller_c scope (outside the function, but within the class definition).</li>  
                    </ol>
                    </li>
                  </ol>
                  <strong>Expected outcome: an evidence-backed threshold interval</strong>
                  <p>
                    You should have a test that sweeps small commands without blocking and a defensible rule for separating a twitch from sustained movement.
                  </p>
