          <article id="exercise-04" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">
              04
            </div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div>
                  <span class="concept-tag">
                    Actuator limit · static test
                  </span>
                  <h2>
                    Saturation: find where power does not increase speed
                  </h2>
                </div>
                <span class="time-badge">
                  20–30 min
                </span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
              
              <aside class="teaching-callout">
                <h3>Motor burn out</h3>
                <p>In this exercise you are guided to run the motors at the upper limits of wheel speed (e.g., fast motors).  If you leave a motor operating at the maximum PWM for a long time, it will get hot and eventually burn out - <strong>causing permanent damage</strong>.  Therefore, you should write code to operate the motors at high speed only for short intermittent periods. </p>
                <p>In application, you will find that the discovered upper limit of motor PWM values produce robot movement that is too fast to be useful.</p> 
                </aside>
                
                <p>
                  When you increase the PWM command to a wheel, you might expect its speed to keep increasing. A real motor system has a finite operating range, so eventually a larger input to the motor may produce little or no repeatable increase in output. There are also other factors that must be considered.  An electronic motor can have varied electrical efficiency across it's input range or with respect to the load it experiences (for more insight, review the plots in the <a href="https://www.pololu.com/file/0J1487/pololu-micro-metal-gearmotors-rev-6-2.pdf">official motor datasheet</a>).  <strong>Saturation</strong> describes this loss of output response to further input. </p>
                  
                  In this exercise, your goal is to determine whether saturation is visible in your measurements and to state the conditions under which you observed it.  On a command-versus-speed plot, saturation may appear as a plateau (a flat portion of the plot).  The fastest output response of the motor is not by itself evidence of saturation. A supported claim of saturation needs a repeatable flattening across several PWM command levels.  </p>
                
					<figure class="motor-diagram">
                <figcaption>
                  Figure 2. An idealised motor response in which settled wheel speed reaches a plateau; the saturation boundary is illustrative
                </figcaption>
                <img class="saturation-illustration" src="assets/images/motors/motor_saturation.png" width="100%" alt="Plot of normalised settled wheel speed against requested motor PWM from zero to 400. An idealised response rises with PWM before levelling at an illustrative saturation boundary. A dashed line shows the proportional response that would continue without a limit, and the shaded high-command region indicates that additional PWM produces little or no additional settled speed." />
              </figure>
                <p>
                  Observing a plateau in output does not necessarily reveal its cause. Experimentally, we need to construct an argument for the most likely causes.  Possible motor-system limits include the motor driver, supply voltage and current, the motor and gearbox, battery condition, and the load at the wheel. Friction, wheel contact, floor surface, and payload can change the response too. These are competing explanations to test.  In these exercises, we can also adopt some sensible assumptions.  For example, for this class of robot, we expect it to operate on a level plane and a smooth surface.
                </p>
                <p>
                  This upper limit matters to motion control. If a controller asks an already saturated motor for more output speed, the motor has no further capability in reality and cannot increase.  For a two-wheel differential steer robot, if one wheel is not able to respond as expected, the total motion of the robot is likely to behave unexpectedly.  Therefore, knowing saturation limits can be accounted for in better control schemes.  In the context of this course, we also want to ensure that the Digital Twin simulation is an accurate and useful model of the real robot system.  If we do not, any behaviours or parameters determined in simulation are unlikely to have utility in reality.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Exercise 3 tasked you with measuring the wheel speed of a motor across a set of discrete PWM values. In this exercise you can reuse that dataset.  However, similar to exercise 3, you may need to repeat some tests with improved resolution across a smaller range at the upper limit of the motor operation.
                </p>
                <aside class="teaching-callout">
                <h3>Run away robot</h3>
                <p>When experimenting with high motor speeds, it is very likely that your robot could run away from you and damage itself.  Very common <strong>permanent damage</strong> to the robot occurs when the robot falls from a height and <strong>bends a motor axle</strong>.</p>
                <p>To prevent this, please develop your test function with the robot flipped over, wheels facing up.</p>
                <p>To test high motor speeds in contact with the surface, you should operate the motors in opposite directions.  This way, your robot will spin on the spot, rather than suddenly running away from you.</p> 
                </aside>
              </div>
              
              <div class="exercise-grid">

                <div class="context-box" data-context-box>
                  <div class="context-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Saturation guidance">
                    <button class="context-tab active" data-context="nonai" role="tab" aria-selected="true">
