                  </div>
                  <div class="context-panel active" data-panel="nonai">
<h3>
                    Investigation brief
                  </h3>
                  <ol>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Start from Exercise 3.</strong> Keep your deadband test function from Exercise 3 and speed-measurement function from Exercise 2. Create a new <code>testSaturation(Robot_c &amp;robot, unsigned long now)</code> function.  In this exercise, we will re-use the same operation in principle.  However, you should adjust your values of \(PWM_{min}\), \(PWM_{max}\) and \(\Delta PWM\) to investigate the upper region of PWM.   
                      <ol>
                      <li>Unlike deadband, it is not necessary for your robot to come to a complete stop after each PWM test.  However, you may need a <strong>settling period</strong> when a new PWM is set, before the test period is used to take measurements. This should ensure that a stable speed is achieved once a PWM value is set. To implement this, you could reuse \(T_{rest}\) as \(T_{settle}\).</li>
                      </ol>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Select the new test.</strong> Call only <code>testSaturation(robot, now)</code> from the Controller_c <code>update(...)</code> function.
                      <ol>
                      <li>To begin with, test your function is operating as expected and reporting useful results.  To conduct this quick test, you can flip your robot upside down.</li>
                      <li>Once you are confident with your test performance, a sensible way to test the upper limits of wheel speed are to command one motor backwards and one forwards.  This way, the robot will spin on the spot, and not run away from you.  Robots falling off tables is a very common cause of permanent damage.</li>
                      </ol>
                      </li>
                      
                    <li>
                      <strong>Calculate and plot.</strong> Plot settled encoder speed against requested PWM. Calculate the change in speed divided by the change in PWM for neighbouring points. A sustained reduction towards zero slope is evidence for saturation.
                    </li>
                  </ol>
                  
                  <strong>Expected outcome: an upper operating-range estimate</strong>
                  <p>
                    You should have a command-versus-settled-speed plot and a justified statement about whether a saturation region was observed within the range you safely tested.
                  </p>
