          <article id="exercise-05" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">
              05
            </div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div>
                  <span class="concept-tag">
                    Response scale · static test
                  </span>
                  <h2>
                    Gain: measure the response slope
                  </h2>
                </div>
                <span class="time-badge">
                  30–40 min
                </span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                  Once the deadband and saturation regions are known, the next question is how strongly the wheel responds within the usable range between them. This response scale is described by the
                  <strong>gain</strong>. For a motor, gain tells us how much the wheel speed changes for a change in requested PWM. A larger gain means that the same PWM step produces a larger change in wheel speed.  In this exercise, we will determine a gain with the assumption that the motor response behaves linearly between input PWM and output wheel speed.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Let \(u\) be requested motor PWM and let \(\hat{\omega}\) be the model's predicted output wheel speed. An ideal proportional model would be:
                </p>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Predicted settled encoder speed omega hat equals gain G multiplied by requested PWM u">
                  \[
                    \hat{\omega}=Gu
                  \]
                </div>
                <p>
                  Here, \(G\) is the gain, a conversion factor whose units depend on how command and wheel speed are represented.  For example, if the \(u\) was measured in encoder counts per second, then a value \(G=4\) would predict that increasing \(u\) by 25 PWM changes the output wheel speed by \(4\times25=100\) encoder counts per second. 
                </p>
                <figure class="motor-diagram">
                <figcaption>
                  Figure 3a. An illustrative local gain fit. The points and region boundaries are synthetic examples, not expected robot values. The line is fitted only to the green points; the orange point is held back for validation.
                </figcaption>
                <img class="gain-illustration" src="./assets/images/motors/motor_gain_linear_fit.png" width="100%" alt="Synthetic settled encoder-speed measurements against requested PWM. Green points inside the selected usable range define a local straight-line fit, an orange point is held back, and grey and amber bands mark illustrative deadband and saturation regions." />
              </figure>
                <p>
                  We know from previous exercises that the motors exhibit a deadband.  That means that the output response of the motor will not pass through the origin on a plot. A more useful model is therefore the equation of a straight line:
                </p>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Predicted settled encoder speed omega hat equals slope G multiplied by requested PWM u plus intercept c">
                  \[
                    \hat{\omega}=Gu+c
                  \]
                </div>
                <p>
                  We can interpret this equation in the context of our robotic application: \(G\) represents the slope of the motor response, whilst \(c\) adjusts for the point at which a given PWM coincides with no motor output speed.  In this exercise we will experimentally determine a value for \(G\) and (\c\).  It is important to recognise that any variation in the task or environment will effect the response of the motor. It is therefore more correct to refer to \(G\) as an <strong>operational gain</strong> (correct within specific operational constraints). The gain we will determine is not intrinsic to the motor.</p>
                
                <p>
                During this exercise, you may notice that your motor response is non-linear.  Exercise 6 investigates non-linearity specifically.  A linear model can still be useful.  In a limited region, a curve may be approximated by a local linear slope.  By completing both exercises 5 and 6, you will be able to make an informed choice about which model is better suited to your application, and whether a more sophisticated model is worth the added complexity.
                </p>
                <p>Once you have collected speed measurements for your robot over a range of PWM, we will plot the result and conduct a linear fit.  With a linear fit equation, each original data point (measured wheel speed) can be evaluated against the line of fit. To do this, for each PWM observation calculate the measured speed minus predicted speed, \(\omega-\hat{\omega}\).  The results of this simple calculation is called a <strong>residual</strong>.</p>
                              <figure class="motor-diagram">
                <figcaption>
                  Figure 3b. Residuals from the same illustrative fit, calculated over the complete measured command range. Their systematic pattern outside the fitting region shows where one local gain no longer describes the response well.
                </figcaption>
                <img class="gain-illustration" src="./assets/images/motors/motor_gain_residuals.png" width="100%" alt="Residuals calculated as measured settled speed minus locally predicted speed over the complete command range. Residuals remain near zero in the selected fitting region but form clear patterns in the illustrative deadband and saturation regions." />
              </figure>  
                <p>
                Plotting the residuals can reveal interesting characteristics for analysis and evaluation.  In the above example plot, the residuals visually communicate where the linear fit is appropriate, and where it is a poor representation.  As expected, the deadband and saturation regions are not well represented by a linear fit - which is clear from large residual values.  In the linear region, residuals that are clustered around a zero value support the approximation of the linear fit.  The distribution of residuals can be analysed more closely to compare one fit against another.
                </p>
                <p>                
                Depending on how much data you collected during exercise 3 Deadband and exercise 4 Saturation, you may be able to combine the datasets to perform a linear fitting process.  If you find that your data in the middle PWM region is sparse, that may be acceptable.  When developing a model of a system, it is possible to intentionally <strong>hold back</strong> some testing intervals to serve as unseen data points to test the predictive power of the model.   
                </p>
              </div>
              

              <div class="exercise-grid">

                <div class="context-box" data-context-box>
                  <div class="context-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Gain guidance">
                    <button class="context-tab active" data-context="nonai" role="tab" aria-selected="true">
