                    </button>
                  </div>
                  <div class="context-panel active" data-panel="nonai">
<h3>
                    Investigation brief
                  </h3>
                  <ol>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Start from your usable range.</strong> Retain the earlier command sweeps and create <code>testGain(Robot_c &amp;robot, unsigned long now)</code>. Use the deadband and saturation evidence from Exercises 3 and 4 to propose a full set of PWM conditions. Select at least one condition in advance to hold back from model fitting.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Select and run the test.</strong> Call only <code>testGain(robot, now)</code> from <code>update(...)</code>. Reuse your non-blocking command, settle, measure, stop, and record phases with the existing <code>TaskTimer_c</code>, <code>robot.setMotorPWM(...)</code>, and cached encoder getters.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Collect consistent evidence.</strong> Work with one wheel and direction first. Measure settled encoder speed at each chosen command and repeat selected points. Keep surface, load, timing, and battery condition as consistent as practical. Save the raw command, speed, wheel, direction, timing, and repeat data rather than retaining only averages.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Fit a local model.</strong> Plot settled speed against PWM before choosing the fitting region. Preserve the full dataset, create a separate fitting subset from the moving, unsaturated region, and fit \(\hat{\omega}=Gu+c\). Report the selected range, slope, intercept, units, repeat variation, and an uncertainty estimate.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Inspect residuals critically.</strong> Calculate \(\omega-\hat{\omega}\) for every measured command and plot these residuals across the complete range. If you adjust the fitting region, record and justify the change. Do not optimise only for a high \(R^2\) over a conveniently small interval.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Predict before testing.</strong> Use the slope and intercept to predict settled speed at the held-back PWM. Test that command under the same conditions and report the signed prediction error. A successful held-out result is stronger evidence than fitting the same point you use to judge the model.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Compare cases.</strong> Repeat for the other wheel and, if time permits, the opposite direction. Decide whether separate gains improve prediction enough to justify the extra parameters in your digital twin.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Check the outcome.</strong> Your code should automate consistent measurements; the response may support a broad or narrow local linear region; and your evidence should include raw records, the fitted range, model coefficients with units, residuals, uncertainty, and held-out prediction. The magnitude and appearance of differences will vary between teams.
                    </li>
                  </ol>
                  <strong>Expected outcome: a tested response slope</strong>
