          <article id="exercise-06" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">
              06
            </div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div>
                  <span class="concept-tag">
                    Response shape · static test
                  </span>
                  <h2>
                    Non-linearity
                  </h2>
                </div>
                <span class="time-badge">
                  25–35 min
                </span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                  <strong>Goal:</strong> determine whether the straight-line gain model developed in Exercise 5 is adequate across the usable motor range, and whether either of two simple non-linear models makes better predictions for commands that were not used to fit any model.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Exercise 5 treated gain as a local slope. If the response were linear across the whole usable range, equal increases in requested PWM would produce approximately equal increases in settled encoder speed. A <strong>non-linear response</strong> has a changing slope: the response may be relatively flat at lower commands and steeper later, or it may rise quickly and then flatten. This is different from saturation, where further command produces little or no additional response at the upper boundary. A curve can contain both a non-linear moving region and a separate saturation plateau.
                </p>
                <p>
                  The residual plot from Exercise 5 provides a useful starting point. Residuals that are small and irregular across the usable range support a straight-line approximation. A repeatable sequence of residuals that begins on one side of zero, crosses it, and then moves to the other side suggests that the line is missing a curved structure. One unusual measurement is not sufficient: changed battery condition, load, wheel contact, settling time, deadband, saturation, or measurement variation can produce a similar visual impression.
                </p>
                <p>
                  We will compare two simple curved candidates with the straight-line model. The first is a <strong>power law</strong>. Let \(x\) be a normalised requested PWM and let \(\hat{v}\) be the predicted normalised settled speed:
                </p>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Predicted normalised settled speed v hat equals normalised requested PWM x raised to gamma">
                  \[
                    \hat{v}=x^\gamma
                  \]
                </div>
                <p>
                  The exponent \(\gamma\), pronounced “gamma”, controls the curve. When \(\gamma=1\), the power law has a linear shape. When \(\gamma>1\), the predicted response is flatter at lower commands and becomes steeper later. When \(0<\gamma<1\), it rises more sharply at first and then flattens. Figure 4a shows these different shapes. Gamma describes a fitted relationship; it is not evidence that the motor contains a physical component or hidden parameter called gamma.
                </p>
                <figure class="motor-diagram">
                  <figcaption>
                    Figure 4a. Illustrative power-law shapes. These curves explain the effect of gamma; they are not measurements or expected parameter values for the robot.
                  </figcaption>
                  <img class="nonlinearity-illustration" src="./assets/images/motors/motor_nonlinearity_candidate_shapes.png" alt="Three illustrative power-law curves of predicted normalised settled speed against normalised requested PWM. Gamma equal to one is linear, gamma below one rises quickly and then flattens, and gamma above one is flatter initially and then steepens." />
                </figure>
                <p>
                  The second candidate is a <strong>second-order polynomial</strong>, also called a quadratic model. It extends a straight line by adding a squared-command term:
                </p>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="A linear model predicts v hat equals A plus B x; a quadratic model predicts v hat equals A plus B x plus C x squared">
                  \[
                    \begin{aligned}
                    \text{linear:}\quad &\hat{v}=A+Bx\\
                    \text{quadratic:}\quad &\hat{v}=A+Bx+Cx^2
                    \end{aligned}
                  \]
                </div>
                <p>
                  In both equations, \(A\) is the intercept and \(B\) describes the linear trend. The additional coefficient \(C\) allows the quadratic slope to change with command: its sign determines the direction of curvature and its magnitude controls how strongly the curve bends. A complete quadratic graph is a parabola, but the usable motor range may show only a gentle segment of that shape. The equation is therefore a flexible candidate description, not a claim that one quadratic physical mechanism causes the response.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Normalisation places command and speed on comparable scales before fitting the power law. Let \(u_{\min}\) and \(u_{\max}\) be the lower and upper boundaries of the usable command range established from your deadband and saturation evidence. Let \(\omega_{\min}\) and \(\omega_{\max}\) be the corresponding operational settled-speed reference values. Create derived columns:
                </p>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Normalised requested PWM x equals u minus u minimum divided by u maximum minus u minimum; normalised settled speed v equals omega minus omega minimum divided by omega maximum minus omega minimum">
                  \[
                    x=\frac{u-u_{\min}}{u_{\max}-u_{\min}},
                    \qquad
                    v=\frac{\omega-\omega_{\min}}
                    {\omega_{\max}-\omega_{\min}}
                  \]
                </div>
                <p>
                  Within the stated boundaries, \(x\) and \(v\) are intended to run approximately from 0 to 1. Keep the requested PWM \(u\) and measured encoder speed \(\omega\) unchanged in the raw dataset and add \(x\) and \(v\) only for analysis. Normalisation changes the numerical scale; it does not calibrate PWM into voltage, current, torque, or mechanical power, and it does not remove uncertainty in the estimated boundaries.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Compare the linear, quadratic, and power-law candidates using exactly the same fitting observations and the same held-back commands. For observation \(k\), calculate the normalised residual \(e_k=v_k-\hat{v}_k\). You can summarise prediction error using mean absolute error:
                </p>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Mean absolute error equals one over N multiplied by the sum of the absolute measured normalised speed minus predicted normalised speed">
                  \[
                    e_k=v_k-\hat{v}_k,
                    \qquad
                    \operatorname{MAE}
                    =\frac{1}{N}\sum_{k=1}^{N}|v_k-\hat{v}_k|
                  \]
                </div>
                <p>
                  Figure 4b illustrates this comparison. The residual patterns reveal structure hidden by the overlaid curves, while the shaded held-back commands test prediction rather than fit. A quadratic has more adjustable coefficients than the power law and can often follow fitting data more closely. That extra flexibility is useful only if it gives repeatably better predictions; otherwise it may be fitting measurement variation. Prefer the simplest model that predicts adequately over the range needed by your digital twin.
                </p>
                <figure class="motor-diagram">
                  <figcaption>
                    Figure 4b. Three candidate models fitted to the same synthetic motor observations, with two commands held back from every fit. The lower panel compares residual structure. The result illustrates the method, not which model a team should expect to select.
                  </figcaption>
                  <img class="nonlinearity-illustration" src="./assets/images/motors/motor_nonlinearity_model_comparison.png" alt="Two-panel illustrative motor model comparison. The upper panel shows synthetic normalised speed observations with fitted linear, quadratic, and power-law curves; amber bands and orange diamonds identify two held-back commands. The lower panel shows residuals for all three models across the same command range." />
                </figure>
                <p>
                  Begin with the raw measurements retained from Exercise 5. If their command spacing is too coarse to resolve the apparent bend, collect additional observations only where needed and keep the new trial identifiable rather than silently merging measurements taken under changed conditions. Analyse one wheel and direction first, then repeat the same comparison for the other cases. A defensible conclusion may retain the straight line, support one curved candidate only over a stated range, require different models by wheel or direction, or reject all three candidates.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="exercise-grid">

                <div class="context-box" data-context-box>
                  <div class="context-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Non-linearity guidance">
                    <button class="context-tab active" data-context="nonai" role="tab" aria-selected="true">
