                  </h3>
                  <ol>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Review the gain evidence.</strong> Begin with the complete raw dataset, fitted range, and residuals retained from Exercise 5. Identify any repeatable residual pattern and predict whether a linear, quadratic, or power-law candidate is likely to describe it. Keep the held-back commands separate from every fitting process.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Decide whether more measurements are needed.</strong> Reuse the Exercise 5 records if they span the usable range with sufficient resolution and consistent conditions. If the commands are too widely spaced to locate a bend, keep <code>testGain(...)</code> and create <code>testResponseShape(Robot_c &amp;robot, unsigned long now)</code> to add targeted points. Do not silently merge trials if battery, surface, load, timing, or apparatus conditions changed.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Collect targeted evidence.</strong> Call only <code>testResponseShape(robot, now)</code> from <code>update(...)</code> when collecting new data. Begin with one wheel and direction, reuse the established non-blocking settle-and-measure sequence, repeat selected commands, and retain trial labels and raw observations. Exclude deadband and saturation from the response-shape fit rather than treating their boundaries as curvature.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Add normalised analysis columns.</strong> State \(u_{\min}\), \(u_{\max}\), \(\omega_{\min}\), and \(\omega_{\max}\), including how they were estimated. Preserve requested PWM and encoder speed, then calculate \(x\) and \(v\) in new columns. Use the same fixed normalisation constants for fitting and held-back observations.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Compare three candidates fairly.</strong> Fit the linear, quadratic, and power-law models using the same observations. Record \(A\), \(B\), \(C\), and \(\gamma\) where applicable, plot all predictions against the normalised measurements, and calculate \(v-\hat{v}\) and MAE using the same definition for every model.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Test held-back predictions.</strong> Freeze the fitted coefficients before revealing the reserved measurements. Compare predicted and observed speeds at each held-back command, report both normalised and original encoder-speed errors, and check whether the ranking of the models agrees with the fitting residuals.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Test the scope.</strong> Repeat the same comparison for the other wheel and, where practical, the opposite direction. Decide whether one shared response shape is adequate or whether separate models improve held-back prediction enough to justify their extra complexity.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Check the outcome.</strong> Your evidence should contain retained raw data, stated operating boundaries, derived normalised columns, all three candidate fits, residual plots, the same error metric, coefficient uncertainty, and held-back prediction errors. It is valid to retain the straight line or conclude that none of the candidates is adequate. The response will vary between teams, wheels, directions, and physical conditions.
                    </li>
                  </ol>
                  <strong>Pseudocode before C++</strong>
