          <article id="exercise-07" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">
              07
            </div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div>
                  <span class="concept-tag">
                    Actuator dynamics · step test
                  </span>
                  <h2>
                    Slew-rate and lag
                  </h2>
                </div>
                <span class="time-badge">
                  35–45 min
                </span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                  Exercises 5 and 6 used requested PWM to predict a <strong>settled</strong> wheel speed. That static relationship identifies the destination of a change, but not how the wheel gets there. When a command changes, motor current, the rotor, gearbox, wheel, robot body, and any embedded processing all take time to respond. A digital twin that jumps immediately to the new settled speed may therefore predict the final motion correctly while predicting the path, stopping distance, or controller timing poorly.
                </p>
                <p>
                  A useful experiment is a <strong>step test</strong>: hold one command until the wheel speed is steady, change the command once, then retain the complete time series until the new speed settles. Let \(\omega_0\) be the initial speed and let \(\omega_\infty\) be the final settled speed predicted by the static model. Several simple temporal models could connect these two values; they are candidate descriptions to test, not mechanisms to assume.
                </p>
                <p>
                  In a <strong>slew-rate model</strong>, wheel speed cannot change faster than a limiting rate \(S\):
                </p>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="The magnitude of the predicted wheel-speed rate of change is less than or equal to the slew-rate limit S">
                  <span>
                    \(|d\hat{\omega}/dt|\)
                  </span>
                  <b>
                    ≤
                  </b>
                  <span>
                    \(S\)
                  </span>
                </div>
                <p>
                  Here, \(\hat{\omega}\) is predicted encoder speed and \(S\) has units of encoder-speed change per second, such as counts s<sup>−2</sup>. A sufficiently large command step then produces an approximately straight ramp whose slope is bounded by \(S\). The same limit need not describe rising and falling responses, both wheels, or both directions.
                </p>
                <p>
                  A different candidate is a <strong>first-order lag</strong>. Its rate of change is proportional to the remaining distance from the final speed:
                </p>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Time constant tau multiplied by the rate of change of predicted wheel speed equals final settled speed minus predicted wheel speed">
                  <span>
                    \(\tau\,d\hat{\omega}/dt\)
                  </span>
                  <b>
                    =
                  </b>
                  <span>
                    \(\omega_\infty-\hat{\omega}\)
                  </span>
                </div>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Predicted wheel speed after a step equals initial speed plus the total speed change multiplied by one minus e raised to negative elapsed time divided by tau">
                  <span>
                    \(\hat{\omega}(t)\)
                  </span>
                  <b>
                    =
                  </b>
                  <span>
                    \(\omega_0+(\omega_\infty-\omega_0)(1-e^{-(t-t_0)/\tau})\)
                  </span>
                </div>
                <p>
                  The time constant \(\tau\) sets the response timescale. For a pure first-order step, the prediction has completed about 63.2% of its total change at \(t=t_0+\tau\). This shortcut is not a general definition of motor response time: it is only a valid estimate of \(\tau\) when a first-order model is adequate and a slew limit is not controlling the early response.
                </p>
              </div>
              <figure class="motor-diagram">
                <figcaption>
                  Figure 5a. Candidate responses to the same illustrative command step
                </figcaption>
                <img class="dynamics-illustration" src="./assets/images/motors/motor_dynamics_candidate_responses.png" alt="Four synthetic normalised wheel-speed responses to the same command step. An instantaneous candidate jumps to the final response, a slew-limited candidate rises as a straight ramp, a first-order lag curves towards the final response, and a combined candidate begins gradually before approaching the same endpoint." />
              </figure>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                  Figure 5a shows why a single rise-time value is not enough. A slew-limited response has an approximately constant slope while the limit is active. A first-order response changes fastest initially, then its slope decreases as it approaches \(\omega_\infty\). A combined model may begin with pronounced curvature and take longer to settle. At a slow sampling or reporting rate these shapes can look deceptively similar, so the evidence must include timestamps and more than a few points across the transient.
                </p>
                <p>
                  The measured trace also includes the <strong>measurement pipeline</strong>. The supplied controller requests a new cached encoder snapshot on a nominal 10 ms update cycle, whereas <code>publishTelemetry(...)</code> emits records on a separate nominal 50 ms cycle. Wheel speed is itself calculated from a count difference over a time interval. That interval, irregular execution timing, delayed records, or additional averaging can smooth a sharp change. Record the actual elapsed time for every successful snapshot, and distinguish acquisition, speed calculation, and reporting before interpreting a curve as motor physics.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Compare every candidate against the same observations. Fit parameters using one or more repeated steps, plot residuals against elapsed time, and look for structure rather than relying on a fit score alone. Then freeze the parameters and predict a new transition—for example, a different step size or a falling step. Figure 5b illustrates this logic with synthetic data. It does not show the response students should expect: its purpose is to demonstrate how a model that follows one trace can still fail on a genuinely new transition.
                </p>
              </div>
              <figure class="motor-diagram">
                <figcaption>
                  Figure 5b. Illustrative fitting, residuals, and held-back prediction
                </figcaption>
                <img class="dynamics-illustration" src="./assets/images/motors/motor_dynamics_model_comparison.png" alt="Two-column synthetic model comparison. A rising step is used to fit slew-only, lag-only, and combined candidates, with residuals shown below. The frozen models then predict a held-back falling step, where the residual patterns reveal different predictive performance." />
              </figure>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p>
                  Choose steps inside the moving, unsaturated range established earlier, so deadband and saturation do not dominate the transient. Begin and end each trial with a long enough hold to estimate \(\omega_0\) and \(\omega_\infty\), repeat selected trials, and preserve wheel, direction, command, timestamps, counts, calculated speed, and trial identity. A clear slew limit, a clear first-order lag, a combination, or no resolvable delay at the available sampling rate are all legitimate evidence-led outcomes. State the tested range and resolution rather than converting an unresolved effect into a fitted certainty.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="exercise-grid">

                <div class="context-box" data-context-box>
                  <div class="context-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Dynamics guidance">
                    <button class="context-tab active" data-context="nonai" role="tab" aria-selected="true">
                      Self-Directed Route
                    </button>
                    <button class="context-tab" data-context="ai" role="tab" aria-selected="false">
                      AI-Assisted Route
                    </button>
                  </div>
                  <div class="context-panel active" data-panel="nonai">
                  <h3>
                    Investigation brief
                  </h3>
                  <ol>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Define the comparison before collecting data.</strong> Retain the earlier functions and create <code>testStepResponse(Robot_c &amp;robot, unsigned long now)</code>. Use the deadband, saturation, gain, and response-shape evidence from Exercises 3–6 to choose initial and final commands inside a moving, unsaturated range. Write down the instantaneous, slew-only, first-order, and combined candidates you intend to test.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Implement one non-blocking step.</strong> Call only <code>testStepResponse(...)</code> from <code>update(...)</code>. Use a test phase and <code>TaskTimer_c</code> to hold the initial command, apply the new command once, retain timestamped encoder snapshots while the response changes, hold the final command, and stop safely. Keep command generation independent from telemetry so reporting cannot change the planned step timing.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Pilot the measurement timing.</strong> Preserve the actual timestamp and encoder count at each accepted snapshot, then calculate speed using the measured elapsed interval. Check how often snapshots are acquired, speed is calculated, and records are reported. Plot an unaveraged pilot trace before deciding whether the transient is resolved adequately; do not treat the nominal task interval as the observed interval.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Collect repeated transitions.</strong> Begin with a moderate rising step and repeat it under comparable conditions. Then collect at least one different step size and one falling step if it is safe to do so. Work with one wheel and direction first; label every trial, and retain raw records rather than only an average curve.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Fit competing models.</strong> Plot requested command and measured speed against elapsed time. Fit the slew-only and first-order candidates to the same fitting trials, and add a combined model only if the additional parameter is justified. Plot residuals against time, compare repeated trials, and report parameter uncertainty or sensitivity. Use the 63.2% shortcut for \(\tau\) only if the response is consistent with a first-order model and is not initially controlled by a slew limit.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>Predict a held-back step.</strong> Freeze the selected parameters before using the reserved transition. Predict its complete time response, compare prediction with observation, and report residuals in the original speed units. A new step size or direction is a stronger test than withholding a few samples from the same trace.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <strong>State what the evidence resolves.</strong> Report the wheel, direction, command range, load and surface conditions, acquisition and reporting timing, repeats, model equations, parameter estimates, uncertainty, residual patterns, and held-back error. Conclude that the response is consistent with slew limiting, first-order lag, both, an alternative, or that the available measurements do not resolve the distinction.
                    </li>
                  </ol>
                  <strong>Pseudocode before C++</strong>
                  <pre><code>CHOOSE initial and final commands from the usable range
HOLD the initial command until speed is steady
AT the planned step time, change the command once
ON each accepted encoder snapshot:
    retain trial, command, timestamp, and raw counts
HOLD until the final speed is steady, then STOP safely
CALCULATE speed from count and actual-time differences
FIT candidates to designated trials and inspect residuals
FREEZE the parameters, then predict the held-back transition</code></pre>
                  <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Speed change rate equals change in encoder speed divided by change in time">
                    <span>speed-change rate</span><b>=</b><span>change in encoder speed / change in time</span>
                  </div>
                  <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Residual at each time equals measured wheel speed minus predicted wheel speed">
                    <span>residual at time \(t\)</span><b>=</b><span>\(\omega(t)-\hat{\omega}(t)\)</span>
                  </div>
                  <p>
                    <a class="template-link" href="./assets/images/motors/generate_motor_dynamics_plots.py" download>Download the Python and Seaborn source for Figures 5a and 5b</a>
                  </p>
                  <strong>Expected outcome: a timestamped dynamic response</strong>
                  <p>
                    You should finish with a reproducible step-test dataset and a model comparison that distinguishes what happened immediately after a command change from the settled response measured earlier. A justified detection limit or rejection of all proposed models is a valid result.
                  </p>
                  </div>
                  <div class="context-panel" data-panel="ai" hidden>
                    <strong>
                      AI-assisted option
                    </strong>
                    <p>
                      Ask AI to help preserve and plot your timestamped data, fit the same candidate equations to the same trials, and prepare a held-back prediction. Require it to separate raw columns from derived speed, identify the fitting trials, show units and residuals, and state how irregular timestamps were handled. Independently check the code, equations, and parameter interpretation against your own trace.
                    </p>
                    <div class="prompt-snippet">
                      “Preserve these raw timestamps, encoder counts, commands, and trial labels. Calculate wheel speed using actual elapsed time. Fit slew-only and first-order-lag candidates to the same named fitting trials, add a combined candidate only if justified, plot residuals, report uncertainty, then use frozen parameters to predict this held-back transition. Do not invent measurements or assume which model generated the data.”
                    </div>
                    <span class="ai-reminder">
                      AI can compare models; only your physical measurements can support the conclusion.
                    </span>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="investigation exercise-evidence">
                  <div class="measure-row">
                    <span>
